    {
      "id": "hSEBf6BcpMwz5J1sIqgd7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 294.60363812156766,
      "y": 692.62109375,
      "width": 1188.4991455078125,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 137830031,
      "version": 470,
      "versionNonce": 1233170525,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785524067553,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "**** CORRECTNESS ****\nPrevent data corruption when multiple thread access shared states\n====================\n-- Eg: in ticket event, both user check for ticket availability before either completed \n-- Coarse-grained: locking protects all related state with one lock\n-- Fine-grained locking: allows concurrent access to independent resources while protecting related ones\n-- Atomic variables: work for single variables but fail for multi-field invariants\n-- Thread confinement: eliminates concurrency entirely for related data\n-- 2 patterns:\n**** Check-then-act: like booking a seat if available\n**** Read-modify-write: like incrementing a counter\n\nExample:\n** Hit Counter: You're counting page views. Two requests hit simultaneously and both read the current value of 1000,\n both add 1 to get 1001, and both write 1001 back. You're now at 1001 when you should be at 1002. \nYou've lost a page view. Over millions of requests, your analytics are way off. \nGiven it's a single variable, use an atomic long with an atomic increment operation.\n------------------\n** Bank Account: A user deposits $50 from their phone while their $500 paycheck direct deposit hits at the same time. \nBoth threads read the balance as $100. The phone deposit computes $100 + $50 = $150 and writes it. \nThe direct deposit computes $100 + $500 = $600 and writes it. \nFinal balance is $600 when it should be $650—the $50 deposit vanished. \nUse a lock around the read-add-write sequence so only one deposit runs at a time.\n-------------------------\n** Metrics Aggregator: You're tracking response times with a running sum and count. \nTwo requests finish at once and both read sum=5000ms and count=100. \nBoth add their response time (say, 50ms each) and increment count. \nBoth write sum=5050 and count=101. You should have sum=5100 and count=102, \nbut one measurement is gone. Again, since it's multiple variables, use a lock to update sum and count together.\n----------------------------\n** Inventory System: Two fans buy the last t-shirt simultaneously. \nBoth read quantity as 1, both subtract 1 to get 0, both write 0. \nYou've sold two shirts but only decremented once—now you've oversold and one fan gets a refund. \nYou need a lock here because you're checking quantity before decrementing—\nthat's check-then-act and read-modify-write combined.\n--------------------------------\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "**** CORRECTNESS ****\nPrevent data corruption when multiple thread access shared states\n====================\n-- Eg: in ticket event, both user check for ticket availability before either completed \n-- Coarse-grained: locking protects all related state with one lock\n-- Fine-grained locking: allows concurrent access to independent resources while protecting related ones\n-- Atomic variables: work for single variables but fail for multi-field invariants\n-- Thread confinement: eliminates concurrency entirely for related data\n-- 2 patterns:\n**** Check-then-act: like booking a seat if available\n**** Read-modify-write: like incrementing a counter\n\nExample:\n** Hit Counter: You're counting page views. Two requests hit simultaneously and both read the current value of 1000,\n both add 1 to get 1001, and both write 1001 back. You're now at 1001 when you should be at 1002. \nYou've lost a page view. Over millions of requests, your analytics are way off. \nGiven it's a single variable, use an atomic long with an atomic increment operation.\n------------------\n** Bank Account: A user deposits $50 from their phone while their $500 paycheck direct deposit hits at the same time. \nBoth threads read the balance as $100. The phone deposit computes $100 + $50 = $150 and writes it. \nThe direct deposit computes $100 + $500 = $600 and writes it. \nFinal balance is $600 when it should be $650—the $50 deposit vanished. \nUse a lock around the read-add-write sequence so only one deposit runs at a time.\n-------------------------\n** Metrics Aggregator: You're tracking response times with a running sum and count. \nTwo requests finish at once and both read sum=5000ms and count=100. \nBoth add their response time (say, 50ms each) and increment count. \nBoth write sum=5050 and count=101. You should have sum=5100 and count=102, \nbut one measurement is gone. Again, since it's multiple variables, use a lock to update sum and count together.\n----------------------------\n** Inventory System: Two fans buy the last t-shirt simultaneously. \nBoth read quantity as 1, both subtract 1 to get 0, both write 0. \nYou've sold two shirts but only decremented once—now you've oversold and one fan gets a refund. \nYou need a lock here because you're checking quantity before decrementing—\nthat's check-then-act and read-modify-write combined.\n--------------------------------\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
