    {
      "id": "2GfOkPSN6eXaIJzHy7Nz0",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 225.30644139379945,
      "y": 2569.8516140977295,
      "width": 492.4638366699219,
      "height": 70,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2h",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 958361021,
      "version": 90,
      "versionNonce": 1547376915,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785532999219,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "** Pushing via consistent hashes **\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "** Pushing via consistent hashes **\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "fthZsdiulIIHOICQpCkT_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 186.7419142652999,
      "y": 2696.0896919847632,
      "width": 926.83935546875,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2i",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 451913843,
      "version": 342,
      "versionNonce": 385969331,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785532947021,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Simple Hashing (when N server is fixed)\n- Zookeeper or Etcd to manage metadata and userId assignment to serverId\n- We'll make the server number n responsible for user u % N. \nWhen clients initially connect to our service, we can either:\na. Directly connect them to the appropriate server \n(e.g. by having them know the hash, N, and the server addressess associated with each index).\nb. Have them randomly connect to any of the servers and have that server \nredirect them to the appropriate server based on internal data.\n\nSteps by steps:\n\n1. The client connects to a random server.\n2. The server uses Zookeeper's server list to compute which server is responsible \nfor the client (by hashing their ID and applying modulo N).\n3. The server redirects the client to the appropriate server.\n4. The client connects to the correct server.\n5. The server adds that client to a map of connections.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Simple Hashing (when N server is fixed)\n- Zookeeper or Etcd to manage metadata and userId assignment to serverId\n- We'll make the server number n responsible for user u % N. \nWhen clients initially connect to our service, we can either:\na. Directly connect them to the appropriate server \n(e.g. by having them know the hash, N, and the server addressess associated with each index).\nb. Have them randomly connect to any of the servers and have that server \nredirect them to the appropriate server based on internal data.\n\nSteps by steps:\n\n1. The client connects to a random server.\n2. The server uses Zookeeper's server list to compute which server is responsible \nfor the client (by hashing their ID and applying modulo N).\n3. The server redirects the client to the appropriate server.\n4. The client connects to the correct server.\n5. The server adds that client to a map of connections.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ZfKVwS4VIIojAuFeI7WQj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1485.3374499663964,
      "y": 2687.0125181020476,
      "width": 1100.0792236328125,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1175721491,
      "version": 1038,
      "versionNonce": 3803165,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785533320112,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Consistent hashing (supporting dynamic/scaling service)\n- Minimize number of connection that need to move when scaling\n- Map servers and user onto hash ring and user connect to next server\nthey encounter when moving clockwise around the ring\n- Advantage\n* Predictable server assignment\n* Minimal connection disruption during scaling\n* Work well with stateful connection\n- Disadvantage\n* complex to implement, require coordination service (zookeeper)\n* all server need to maintain routing information\n* connection state is lost if server fails\n\nEg:  the Google Docs design, connections are associated with specific documents \nthat require substantial state to maintain collaborative editing functionality. \nLoading a document, applying all pending operations, and syncing with collaborators is expensive.\nConsistent hashing keeps that state on a single server while allowing for scaling.\n\n\n\n\n*** How to scale (orchestration logic)\n1. Signaling the beginning of a scaling event. Recording both the old and new server assignments.\n2. Slowly disconnecting clients from the old server and having them reconnect to their newly assigned server.\n3. Signaling the end of the scaling event and updating the coordination service with the new server assignments.\n4. In the interim, having messages which are sent to both the old and new server until they're fully transitioned.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Consistent hashing (supporting dynamic/scaling service)\n- Minimize number of connection that need to move when scaling\n- Map servers and user onto hash ring and user connect to next server\nthey encounter when moving clockwise around the ring\n- Advantage\n* Predictable server assignment\n* Minimal connection disruption during scaling\n* Work well with stateful connection\n- Disadvantage\n* complex to implement, require coordination service (zookeeper)\n* all server need to maintain routing information\n* connection state is lost if server fails\n\nEg:  the Google Docs design, connections are associated with specific documents \nthat require substantial state to maintain collaborative editing functionality. \nLoading a document, applying all pending operations, and syncing with collaborators is expensive.\nConsistent hashing keeps that state on a single server while allowing for scaling.\n\n\n\n\n*** How to scale (orchestration logic)\n1. Signaling the beginning of a scaling event. Recording both the old and new server assignments.\n2. Slowly disconnecting clients from the old server and having them reconnect to their newly assigned server.\n3. Signaling the end of the scaling event and updating the coordination service with the new server assignments.\n4. In the interim, having messages which are sent to both the old and new server until they're fully transitioned.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
