      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Use Case        Recommended Transport        Communication Pattern        Key Architecture Components        Main Scaling Challenge        Consistency / Conflict Handling        Interview Deep-Dive Points\nChat Applications        WebSocket        Bidirectional, low-latency        WebSocket gateway, chat service, pub/sub broker, message store, presence service        Routing messages across many servers and large group fan-out        Per-conversation sequence number, idempotent message ID, reconnect and missed-message recovery        Message ordering, delivery acknowledgments, offline messages, typing indicators, presence, reconnect, backpressure\nLive Comments        WebSocket or SSE        Mostly server fan-out with client submissions        Ingestion API, moderation pipeline, stream processor, hierarchical pub/sub, regional fan-out servers        Millions of users publishing and receiving comments simultaneously        Global ordering is usually unnecessary; preserve ordering within a channel or partition        Batch comments, sample or rank comments, partition by event, moderation, rate limiting, hot-channel mitigation\nCollaborative Document Editing        WebSocket        High-frequency bidirectional updates        Collaboration gateway, document session service, operation log, snapshot store, presence service        Concurrent edits to the same document and high-frequency character updates        Operational Transformation or CRDTs reconcile concurrent operations and converge document state        Conflict resolution, operation versioning, cursor presence, selection updates, snapshots, reconnect, undo/redo\nLive Dashboards and Analytics        Server-Sent Events        One-way server-to-client stream        Metrics pipeline, stream processor, aggregation service, SSE gateway, cache        Large numbers of dashboards subscribing to frequently changing metrics        Latest-value semantics often matter more than processing every intermediate event        Aggregation intervals, event coalescing, reconnect with Last-Event-ID, stale-data indicators, “real-time enough” SLA\nGaming and Interactive Applications        WebRTC for peer traffic; WebSocket or UDP-based protocol for server coordination        Bidirectional, extremely low-latency        Matchmaking, authoritative game server, state synchronization, relay or TURN servers, regional edge        Maintaining low latency while synchronizing many rapidly changing objects        Authoritative server, prediction, interpolation, reconciliation, tick or sequence numbers        Update frequency by object type, client-side prediction, lag compensation, cheating prevention, packet loss, regional placement\n\nTransport Selection Summary\n\nTransport        Best Fit        Strengths        Limitations\nWebSocket        Chat, collaboration, game coordination        Full-duplex, low latency, persistent connection        Connection management, reconnect logic, stateful infrastructure, backpressure\nServer-Sent Events        Dashboards, notifications, one-way streams        Simple HTTP model, automatic browser reconnect, Last-Event-ID support        Server-to-client only, browser connection limits, text-based\nWebRTC        Peer-to-peer gaming, audio, video, direct data channels        Very low latency, peer-to-peer path, unordered or unreliable delivery options        NAT traversal, TURN cost, complex signaling and debugging\nPolling / Long Polling        Compatibility fallback, low-frequency updates        Simple infrastructure and HTTP semantics        Higher latency and request overhead; inefficient at high update frequency\n\nCollaborative Editing Conflict Resolution\n\nApproach        Core Idea        Advantages        Trade-offs\nOperational Transformation        Transform concurrent operations relative to operations already applied        Proven in centralized document systems; compact operations        Transformation logic becomes complex as operation types grow; usually relies on a central server\nCRDT        Design data structures whose concurrent updates merge deterministically        Supports offline and decentralized editing; eventual convergence        More metadata, memory, and implementation complexity; tombstone or compaction management may be required\n\nExcalidraw Layout\n\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                              REAL-TIME APPLICATION PATTERNS                                  │\n├────────────────────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Use Case           │ Transport       │ Primary Challenge  │ Important Design Topics          │\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Chat               │ WebSocket       │ Fan-out + ordering │ Presence, typing, reconnect       │\n│ Live Comments      │ WS / SSE        │ Extreme fan-out    │ Batching, moderation, hot events  │\n│ Collaborative Docs │ WebSocket       │ Concurrent edits   │ OT/CRDT, cursors, snapshots       │\n│ Live Dashboards    │ SSE             │ Update volume      │ Aggregation, coalescing, resume    │\n│ Gaming             │ WebRTC + WS     │ Ultra-low latency  │ Prediction, ticks, reconciliation │\n└────────────────────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┘",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Use Case        Recommended Transport        Communication Pattern        Key Architecture Components        Main Scaling Challenge        Consistency / Conflict Handling        Interview Deep-Dive Points\nChat Applications        WebSocket        Bidirectional, low-latency        WebSocket gateway, chat service, pub/sub broker, message store, presence service        Routing messages across many servers and large group fan-out        Per-conversation sequence number, idempotent message ID, reconnect and missed-message recovery        Message ordering, delivery acknowledgments, offline messages, typing indicators, presence, reconnect, backpressure\nLive Comments        WebSocket or SSE        Mostly server fan-out with client submissions        Ingestion API, moderation pipeline, stream processor, hierarchical pub/sub, regional fan-out servers        Millions of users publishing and receiving comments simultaneously        Global ordering is usually unnecessary; preserve ordering within a channel or partition        Batch comments, sample or rank comments, partition by event, moderation, rate limiting, hot-channel mitigation\nCollaborative Document Editing        WebSocket        High-frequency bidirectional updates        Collaboration gateway, document session service, operation log, snapshot store, presence service        Concurrent edits to the same document and high-frequency character updates        Operational Transformation or CRDTs reconcile concurrent operations and converge document state        Conflict resolution, operation versioning, cursor presence, selection updates, snapshots, reconnect, undo/redo\nLive Dashboards and Analytics        Server-Sent Events        One-way server-to-client stream        Metrics pipeline, stream processor, aggregation service, SSE gateway, cache        Large numbers of dashboards subscribing to frequently changing metrics        Latest-value semantics often matter more than processing every intermediate event        Aggregation intervals, event coalescing, reconnect with Last-Event-ID, stale-data indicators, “real-time enough” SLA\nGaming and Interactive Applications        WebRTC for peer traffic; WebSocket or UDP-based protocol for server coordination        Bidirectional, extremely low-latency        Matchmaking, authoritative game server, state synchronization, relay or TURN servers, regional edge        Maintaining low latency while synchronizing many rapidly changing objects        Authoritative server, prediction, interpolation, reconciliation, tick or sequence numbers        Update frequency by object type, client-side prediction, lag compensation, cheating prevention, packet loss, regional placement\n\nTransport Selection Summary\n\nTransport        Best Fit        Strengths        Limitations\nWebSocket        Chat, collaboration, game coordination        Full-duplex, low latency, persistent connection        Connection management, reconnect logic, stateful infrastructure, backpressure\nServer-Sent Events        Dashboards, notifications, one-way streams        Simple HTTP model, automatic browser reconnect, Last-Event-ID support        Server-to-client only, browser connection limits, text-based\nWebRTC        Peer-to-peer gaming, audio, video, direct data channels        Very low latency, peer-to-peer path, unordered or unreliable delivery options        NAT traversal, TURN cost, complex signaling and debugging\nPolling / Long Polling        Compatibility fallback, low-frequency updates        Simple infrastructure and HTTP semantics        Higher latency and request overhead; inefficient at high update frequency\n\nCollaborative Editing Conflict Resolution\n\nApproach        Core Idea        Advantages        Trade-offs\nOperational Transformation        Transform concurrent operations relative to operations already applied        Proven in centralized document systems; compact operations        Transformation logic becomes complex as operation types grow; usually relies on a central server\nCRDT        Design data structures whose concurrent updates merge deterministically        Supports offline and decentralized editing; eventual convergence        More metadata, memory, and implementation complexity; tombstone or compaction management may be required\n\nExcalidraw Layout\n\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                              REAL-TIME APPLICATION PATTERNS                                  │\n├────────────────────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Use Case           │ Transport       │ Primary Challenge  │ Important Design Topics          │\n├────────────────────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Chat               │ WebSocket       │ Fan-out + ordering │ Presence, typing, reconnect       │\n│ Live Comments      │ WS / SSE        │ Extreme fan-out    │ Batching, moderation, hot events  │\n│ Collaborative Docs │ WebSocket       │ Concurrent edits   │ OT/CRDT, cursors, snapshots       │\n│ Live Dashboards    │ SSE             │ Update volume      │ Aggregation, coalescing, resume    │\n│ Gaming             │ WebRTC + WS     │ Ultra-low latency  │ Prediction, ticks, reconciliation │\n└────────────────────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┘",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "YrR9KbDKDLXSdDXaowmtI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 159.8164527798025,
      "y": 3984.6448659913813,
      "width": 907.3193359375,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2u",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1933490141,
      "version": 810,
      "versionNonce": 1319061523,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785535016779,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Advantage\n* Manage load on endpoint servers is easy, use load balancer with least connection strategy\n* We can broadcast updates to large number of client efficiently\n* Minimize proliferation of state through system\n\nDisadvantage\n* don't know whether subscribers are connected to endpoint server, or when they disconnect\n* Pub/Sub service becomes single point of failure and bottleneck\n* additional layer of indirection which can add latency\n* Many-many connection between pub/sub service host/server\n\n==> A good choice when need to broadcast to large number of clients, latency < 10ms,\nand if you don't need to respond to connect/disconnect events or maintain state \n ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Advantage\n* Manage load on endpoint servers is easy, use load balancer with least connection strategy\n* We can broadcast updates to large number of client efficiently\n* Minimize proliferation of state through system\n\nDisadvantage\n* don't know whether subscribers are connected to endpoint server, or when they disconnect\n* Pub/Sub service becomes single point of failure and bottleneck\n* additional layer of indirection which can add latency\n* Many-many connection between pub/sub service host/server\n\n==> A good choice when need to broadcast to large number of clients, latency < 10ms,\nand if you don't need to respond to connect/disconnect events or maintain state \n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
